# Vault recycle-bin response-generation correction

This work-only candidate fixes the independently reproduced interface boundary after Restore or permanent deletion refreshes the active entry list. Both handlers recheck the captured bin generation, row attachment and active view before requesting another bin list. Closing the bin or opening a newer bin/preview during that await therefore retains the user's newer interface state. Normal current-bin refresh still works.

The only application change is two `viewCurrent()` → `binCurrent()` checks in `client.mjs`. The five backend/native modules are exact copies of the immutable 546-artifact recycle stage. No production, earlier evidence, shared-host semantics or original history behavior changes. The baseline binds the original 68-test receipt, exact frozen stage manifest and production runtime fingerprints; historical evidence is referenced rather than recursively recopied or rebound to this client.

The reviewer's five regression bodies are byte-identical; their helper has one explicit app import path adaptation. Three originally failed interface assertions concern closing after Restore/Purge and keeping a newer preview; two existing draft/lifecycle controls remain separate. New finite controls check normal Restore/Purge refresh, closing/reopening during a held mutation, and stale-token refusal followed by successful fresh preview. Relevant earlier history/selection/draft/view-cleanup client tests run with owned synthetic encrypted stores and a DOM element model.

Original assembly executions, child HTTP hosts and actual browser checks are zero in this correction campaign. Prior original comparisons and their default KDBX 4.0 previous-parent loss versus conditional KDBX 4.1 retention remain exactly as documented by the original stage. No new original-runtime equality claim is attached to the interface correction. Independent actual UI review remains a separate acceptance step.

The proposed extra same-generation preview diagnostic was invalid: restoring A changes the whole-graph token, so the unrefreshed B preview correctly refuses before any password field is published. Its guard-only and original-source failed captures remain exact and are explicitly classified as diagnostic setup failures. A fresh control asserts that refusal and then successful preview after refresh. No additional bin-interaction counter was implemented. An earlier row-selector setup failure and a preparation directory-copy failure remain separately preserved. The parent-reported platform interruption is recorded as an incomplete attempt, not an implementation failure.

The source-assisted KeePass 2.61.1 scope, GPL-2.0-or-later attribution and preserved original licenses/provenance remain bound through `prior-stage-binding/NOTICE.md.snapshot`, `source-provenance.json.snapshot` and the original immutable stage manifest. Original EXE/DLL/source-ZIP carriers are not copied into this correction stage. Helpers and copied test tooling do not execute in the application. Entry-only recycle/recovery, native UUID/previous-parent/tombstone limitations and all full-product/security/superiority gaps remain unchanged.

Run `node record-regression.mjs <new-owned-run-name>` from this stage with Node 24.16.0. Tests use stage-owned `.local` observations or owned OS temporary data and write no production/results files. The collector binds source snapshots and raw output, checks the original stage and production-five identities before/after, caps direct-child logs and uses a 60-second child deadline. Fingerprint I/O hard deadlines and race-free Windows descendant isolation are not claimed.
